Assignment 5 addition/modifications:

# Modification
The big modification that we made is that the File IO is now being handled inside the controller
instead of the the model. This means that the CollageProjectModel interface from A4 is now a part
of the CollageController interface. This just means the controller now has more things to handle
from the user. This is done because the controller deals with user input and inputting and
outputting to a specific file is something the controller should do and not the model.

Another modification that was made was made was the transparency calculation and modification. This
was originally done ion the pixel class, however, as we realized that the modification happens once
the composite image needs to be retrieved is when the transparency plays a role, so we decided to
make it into a macro as well.

Another modification that was made was in the Layer class to correctly offset the image when an
image was added to the a Layer.

The final modification that we made was we added a make final image method in the model which
returns the composite image as a 2D List of IPixel which is the representation that we had as an
image. We made this method so that the client can easily retrieve the final image so that they can
see the progress that they made on the project that they are working on.

# Additions
We added more macros to the model so that means there are more classes that implement the
MacroCollegeEffects interface. We also added the GUI which has its own interface and implementation.

# DarkenMultiplyBrightenScreenMacro - Class
The DarkenMultiplyBrightenScreenMacro class is a macro that implements the MacroCollageEffects
interface. It allows the user reduce or increase the brightness all the pixels on a layer by
modifying the hue, saturation, and lightness of a pixel.


# InversionDifferenceMacro - Class
The InversionDifferenceMacro class is a macro that implements the MacroCollageEffects interface.
It allows the user to invert the colors of an image on the current layer based on the composite
image below.


# ChangeTransparencyMacro - Class
The ChangeTransparencyMacro class is a macro that implements the MacroCollageEffects interface.
It allows the user to invert the colors of an image on the current layer based on the composite
image below.


# Features - Interface
The Features interface represents the features that are currently supported with the GUI that we
have made. It is used to delegate information from the GUI when the user clicks on something to the
model and return that back to the GUI.


# CollageGUIController - Class
The CollageGUIController class inherits the Features. This class handles the asynchronous
interactions that the user inputs and delegates action to the model to handle the processing part
and send information to the GUI to update it. This class uses CollageController to handle file IO.


# GUIView - Interface
The GUIView interface represents the Graphical User Interface that the user will see and interact
based on the asynchronous inputs.


# JFrameView - Class
The JFrameView class inherits the GUIView. The GUI that the user will interact with and
uses the Features interface as the controller which performs actions from the listeners in the GUI.
It sets up the events will occur once a certain action is performed by the user.


# Main - Class
The Main class is used to run the GUI that we've made.


We used stackoverflow to get the screen size of a screen so that this image frame will change
depending on the resolution of the screen.
We also used stackoverflow to get the file extension by getting the substring of the string after
the period.



Assignment 4:

# Collager - Title

We divided our Collager Application into three sections; adhering to the Model, View, Controller
pattern in Software design pattern.

# IPixel - Interface

The IPixel interface represents the operations that can be performed on a Pixel
We have methods that allow the user to modify the pixel and calculate for the brightness of the
pixel. There are also observers in the pixel class in case the user wants to know the specific
component of the pixels.

# Pixel - Class

The Pixel class represents a single pixel that makes up images. We made our Pixel class backwards
compatible with other forms of Images in case there is no alpha value which we set to 255. We have
one constructor that takes in all 4 components from an image and one take takes only the rgb values.

# ILayer - Interface

The ILayer interface represents operations

# Layer - Class

The Layer class represents a layer that is used on the collage. It has functionality that allows the
user to add an image onto the layer and observers for the user to know what the name of the layer
they are working on as well as the content on the layer so that they can modify it as they see fit.

# Model

We initially had one interface in our model that housed all the methods necessary to run our program
but as we developed more understanding of the requirements, we felt that it would be best to have
two different interfaces because it was made clear to us that we need the two interfaces to do
different things.

They are organized such that they allow for extensibility in the cases where we are asked to
implement more functionality.

# CollageProjectModel - Interface

The CollageProjectModel interface represents file operations that can be performed on model classes
that inherits it. It gives functionality to make, save, and load a project and also the ability to
save an image.

# CollageProject - Interface

The CollageProject interface represents operations that can be done on the application of a collage.
It gives functionality to add a layer, add an image to a layer, and set a filter onto a layer
the project if the user wants to redo their project. Finally, there are also a bunch of
observers that the user can query if they need to use it. This is information the user has already
known from using the methods mentioned above. So, if they need information about something they
forgot, the observers are there to help them.

This interface extends the CollageProjectModel interface so that users can work on this
CollageProject and perform some file operations.

# CollageProjectModelImpl - Class

The CollageProjectModelImpl class is the model of our program. It inherits the CollageProject. This
class is used to make a collage to work on. It has the functionality from CollageProject like
modifying layers that are made and has observers about the project.

# Effects - Package

We have an Effects package that holds the macros that is used in the model.

# MacroCollageEffects - Interface

The MacroCollageEffects interface is our macro and we came to an agreement that since macros are a
scalable way to create a growing extended set of functionality, it would be best to use the command
design pattern to support a set of effects/ filters that our program offers. This way, our design is
loosely coupled and if we have to add more operations(effects), we can just add new classes that
inherits this interface.

It is also a way to ensure that when a new tool/class is added, it doesn't affect the functionality
of the tools/class that already exists.

# BrightenDarkenMacro - Class

The BrightenDarkenMacro class is a macro that implements the MacroCollageEffects interface. It
allows the user reduce or increase the brightness all the pixels on a layer according to either their
value, intensity or luma.

# BulkAssignFilter - Class

The BulkAssignFilter class is a macro that implements the MacroCollageEffects interface. It allows
the user to filter all the pixels on a layer and set them to one of their components(red, green,
blue).

# CollageView - Interface

The CollageView interface represents a way for the user to see what they are working on. Though
currently it is a text-based implementation on the program so there is nothing to see in real time.
So, right now, it has only one method which is to send a message to the user.

# CollageTextView - Class

The CollageTextView class inherits the CollageView. It is used to print messages to the user.

# CollageController - Interface

The CollageController interface represents a controller for the CollageProjectModelImpl. It is used
to run the program which accept user input and perform some actions like loading, creating and
 saving a project,adding layers to a project etc.

# CollageControllerImpl - Class

The CollageControllerImpl class inherits the CollageController. This class is used to
read user input and perform actions accordingly.

# CollageTextMain - Class

The CollageTextMain class is used to accepts command line arguments that allow the program to be
used.



# HOW TO USE

- Our application is used by typing specific commands into the command-line. Below
are the commands that our program supports and how to use them. The commands are
character and case sensitive so be sure to used them appropriately.

To run the program on the terminal, you need to go to the ..\hw4\out\production\hw4 folder and write
java CollageTextMain.

CREATING A PROJECT
# To create a new project, run the new-project command
and give it the height and width of your choice.

For example if you want your project to have a
height of 5 and width of 10 you would:

Do: new-project [height] [width]
    new-project 5 10

LOADING AN EXISTING PROJECT
# To load an existing project from anywhere on your computer into
    the program, run the load-project command and pass in the file path.

For example if you want to load a project called myCollage
from your desktop into the program, you would :

Do: load-project [filePath]
    load-project desktop/myCollage

NOTE: After creating or loading your project, you can now add layers,
      images and transform them to your liking.

ADDING A LAYER
# To add a new layer to your project, run the add-layer command
   and pass in a layer name of your choice.

 For example if you want to create a new layer called 'designs' in
 your existing project, you would:

 Do: add-layer [layerName]
     add-layer designs

NOTE: MAKE SURE EVERY LAYER YOU MAKE HAS A DIFFERENT NAME.

NOTE: Once your layer is created, you can then add Images to it.

 ADDING IMAGES TO YOUR LAYER
# To add an image to your layer, run the add-image-to-layer command
    and provide the name of the layer you want to place that image
    on, as well as the location of the image on your device and
    finally the position you want to place the image at.

For example, if you want to add an image from your desktop called
    'image1' to a layer you created called 'designs' starting from the
    top left corner of your project screen, you would

Do: add-image-to-layer [layer-name] [image-path] [x-pos] [y-pos]
    add-image-to-layer designs desktop/image1 0 0


NOTE: Once your image is successfully on the layer, you
      can add some effects to it.

 # ADDING EFFECTS TO YOUR IMAGES
    To transform any image on a layer,
    run the set-filter command and pass in
    the layer name of the layer which has the image you want
    to transform as well as any of the filter options
    which our program supports.

For example,
    if you want to isolate the red-component of
    the image on the layer called 'designs' you would

    Do: set-filter [set-filter] [filterName]
        set-filter designs red-component


   if you want to isolate the green-component of
   the image on the layer called 'designs' you would

    Do: set-filter [set-filter] [filterName]
        set-filter designs green-component

    if you want to isolate the blue-component of
    the image on the layer called 'designs' you would

     Do: set-filter [set-filter] [filterName]
         set-filter designs blue-component

     if you want to increase the brightness of
     the image on the layer called 'designs' by its
     value, you would

     Do: set-filter [set-filter] [filterName]
         set-filter designs brighten-value


     if you want to increase the brightness of
     the image on the layer called 'designs' by its
     luma, you would

     Do: set-filter [set-filter] [filterName]
         set-filter designs brighten-luma


     if you want to increase the brightness of
     the image on the layer called 'designs' by its
     intensity, you would

     Do: set-filter [set-filter] [filterName]
         set-filter designs brighten-intensity


     if you want to reduce the brightness of
     the image on the layer called 'designs' by its
     value, you would

     Do: set-filter [set-filter] [filterName]
         set-filter designs darken-value

     if you want to reduce the brightness of
     the image on the layer called 'designs' by its
     luma, you would

    Do: set-filter [set-filter] [filterName]
        set-filter designs darken-luma

    If you want to reduce the brightness of
    the image on the layer called 'designs' by its
    intensity, you would

    Do: set-filter [set-filter] [filterName]
        set-filter designs darken-intensity

 NOTE: These are the only filters our program supports currently.

 NOTE: After you apply any of the filters on you image(s),
       you should save the image to make sure your work
       does not get lost.

 SAVING YOUR IMAGES
# To save an image after applying filters, run the save-image
  command and provide the location of where you want this
  new image to be stored.

  For example, to save a final image to the images folder,

  Do: save-image [filePath]
      save-image images/newImage.ppm

  NOTE: MAKE SURE YOUR IMAGES ARE SAVED WITH DIFFERENT NAMES THAN THEIR ORIGINAL.


  SAVING YOUR PROJECT
# To save a project, run the save-project command
  and pass in the path to store the image at.

 Do: save-project [filePath]
     save-project projects/projectName.collage

NOTE: After all work has been done and saved, and you are
      ready to quit the program, run the quit command.

  Do: quit

NOTE FOR GRADER:
Copy and paste the script in the RunScript file into the command line.
The actions will be performed and the new images and project will be
saved in the specified path for viewing.

At least one example PPM image (different from any of the ones provided to you in this assignment),
its red, blue, and green components as their own images, and this image brightened and darkened
by itself using each of value, intensity, and luma in a second new layer
named suitably in a res/folder.


